{"id":"post_aero_refinement","compound_name":"Graphs::Aero::post_aero_refinement","source_path":"post_aero_refinement/post_aero_refinement.json","category":"Smoke","author":"Autodesk","description":"This graph utilizes the post_refine_aero compound to refine an aero volume and add more detail after the simulation.","documentation_file":"post_aero_refinement_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Post Aero Refinement\n\n### What it does\n\nThis graph utilizes the `post_refine_aero` compound to refine an aero volume and add more detail after the simulation.\n\n### Inputs and outputs\n\nInputs required for this example are a live or cached aero simulation. The output is the resulting refined aero simulation volume. \n\n### How it works\n\n- Simply plug the output from a `simulate_aero` node into the `aero_volume` port of the `post_refine_aero` node. Please note that the `source_air` and `collider` (if in use) nodes must also be plugged into the `post_refine_aero` for it to work. You can then select the number of refinement levels above the base resolution (as defined in the `fluid_detail_size` on the `source_air`). Playback and watch the details appear.\n\n- When loading this graph, the active terminal will show the low resolution base simulation. You can then toggle the proxy flag off, and use the other terminal's (`terminal1`) proxy to view the up-rezzed version of the simulation.\n\n- The `post_refine_aero` node also accepts Bifrost aero simulation caches as input, but it will always still require a connection from the original sources/colliders as well.  \n\n- To obtain the results seen in the thumbnail, create and use new infinite light, and set the `refinement_levels` on the `post_refine_aero` to 2.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.16.0.2","bif":"2.16.0.2","fields":"2.16.0.2","fluids":"2.16.0.2","geometries":"2.16.0.2"},"graph_ports":[],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"true"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"true"